                        <div class="border-b border-theme-border/60 pb-3">
                            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-theme-cyan tracking-widest uppercase">Supporting Arguments • Page 3</span>
                            <h2 class="font-cinzel text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-1">
                                ARGUMENT 1: PERMANENT NATURE AND FUTURE CONSEQUENCES
                            </h2>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-theme-card p-4 rounded-xl border-l-2 border-theme-cyan font-playfair italic text-theme-cyan text-lg">
                            "Once it is online, it stays there forever."
                        </div>

                        <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-theme-light/90 leading-relaxed">
                            Whatever students post today—whether it is an inappropriate comment, an embarrassing photo, or sensitive personal data—can be screenshotted, archived, and shared permanently. Universities and future employers frequently check applicants' digital footprints during the screening process. Therefore, students must realize that careless online behavior can permanently damage their academic and career opportunities.
                        </p>

                        <!-- Citation & Clickable External Link -->
                        <div class="bg-theme-panel p-5 rounded-xl border border-theme-border text-xs space-y-3">
                            <div class="flex items-center text-theme-cyan font-semibold text-sm">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark mr-2"></i> eSafety Commissioner Citation
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-theme-muted italic leading-relaxed">
                                According to the eSafety Commissioner, a person's digital reputation can affect friendships, relationships, and future job opportunities. Everything people post, share, like, or comment on contributes to their digital footprint. Even old posts can influence how others judge someone's character.
                            </p>
                            <a href="https://www.esafety.gov.au/key-topics/staying-safe/digital-reputation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg bg-theme-border/40 text-theme-cyan font-semibold hover:bg-theme-accent hover:text-theme-dark transition">
